{
  "schema": "guide-topic/v1",
  "slug": "fits",
  "order": 1,
  "title": "Feature Fitting: Least-Squares, Tangential & Mating Envelopes",
  "subtitle": "Why the mathematical objective you pick—Gaussian average, one-sided datum contact, or minimum circumscribed shaft—changes both size and axis.",
  "phase": "P0–P1a",
  "status": "live-kernel",
  "provisional": true,
  "crates": [
    "fid-fit",
    "fid-solv",
    "fid-num"
  ],
  "spec_nodes": [
    "KRN.LSQ.RND",
    "KRN.LSQ.LIN",
    "KRN.ENV.TAN.PLANE",
    "KRN.ENV.MCC.CYL"
  ],
  "standards": [
    {
      "designator": "ISO 17450-1:2011",
      "topic": "Feature association objectives (Gaussian, Chebyshev, envelope)"
    },
    {
      "designator": "ISO 5459:2011",
      "topic": "Tangential external datum feature simulators"
    },
    {
      "designator": "ASME Y14.5-2018 §5.7 / §7",
      "topic": "Actual mating envelope and datum simulators"
    }
  ],
  "sections": [
    {
      "heading": "Three distinct questions, three distinct fits",
      "paragraphs": [
        "Raw points from a CMM or optical scanner do not have a single diameter or orientation until you choose an association objective. Gaussian least-squares minimizes the sum of squared orthogonal distances from the points to the ideal primitive—it is stable against random sensor noise and serves as the default reference for many dimension checks.",
        "Physical assembly and datum simulation ask a different question. A part resting on a surface plate cannot penetrate the granite: the datum simulator is a tangential plane touching the highest material peaks from the material-free side (ISO 5459:2011). Similarly, the functional size of an external shaft is governed by its Minimum Circumscribed Cylinder—the smallest ideal sleeve that encloses every measured point (ASME Y14.5-2018 Rule #1)."
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "Outlier leverage and impact disclosure",
      "paragraphs": [
        "Because least-squares squares every residual, a single burr, dust speck or bad scan hit pulls both the fitted centre and diameter aggressively. In Metrology Maven, silent point deletion is prohibited: removing any point records the excluded index set, its digest, and an explicit before-and-after impact disclosure (`KRN.OUT`)."
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "Rocking datums and non-uniqueness",
      "paragraphs": [
        "A slightly convex planar face can rest stably on more than one three-point contact triplet—a rocking datum. `fid-fit`'s tangential plane solver (`fit_tan_plane`) searches multiple seeded starts, verifies KKT contact vertices, and emits an explicit `RockReport` whenever competing contact sets lie within `rock_tol`."
      ]
    }
  ],
